  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Hot, humid conditions persist into next week, \nalong with daily chances for shower and thunderstorms.\n\nA very repetitive weather pattern has set up for the next \nseveral days, characterized by warm and humid air from offshore \nhigh pressure and continued impulses of upper level energy. This\nwill yield showers and storms each day through at least early \nnext week, and likely even beyond. \n\nStarting with today, numerous storms are developing along the\nlee trough, as well as a few along the Chesapeake Bay Breeze.\nThe environment is characterized by around 2000-3000 J/kg of\nMLCAPE, but very weak shear (around 20 knots). This is yielding\na few strong storms, but very few that are able to maintain\nthemselves for very long. This is also likely due to meager\nmid-level lapse rates around 5 C/km. This will continue to be \nthe story through this evening. Latest mesoanalysis also depicts\naround 400-600 J/kg of DCAPE, which coincides with max \ndowndraft velocities around 60 mph or so, which is about what is\nbeing seen in the strongest of storms. Expect this threat to \nremain pretty isolated, with most storms through the evening \nproducing primarily heavy rainfall, frequent lightning, and \ngusts around 40 mph. Torrential rainfall rates can be expected \nat times, with PWATs in excess of two inches currently across \nmuch of the area. Storms will gradually come to an end this \nevening as they rain themselves out after sunset. \n\nFriday looks somewhat similar to what we are seeing today,\nalbeit with slightly more shear/forcing. A slightly more potent\npiece of energy looks to move across the area during peak\nheating. This should yield deep-layer shear values closer to 30\nknots perhaps. There will be a similar thermodynamic environment\nin place, with high amounts of CAPE, but DCAPE values similar or\neven a bit less than today. One thing to watch for though is\nthat, thanks to the shortwave, mid-level lapse rates will be in\nthe 6-7 C/km range tomorrow afternoon, which could lead to a\nhigher coverage of stronger updrafts. This would then allow more\nstorms to potentially tap into that peak downdraft potential and\nyield a few more severe storms than we see this afternoon. Would\nalso expect to see some large hail as well, given the higher\nlapse rates aloft. A Marginal Risk is maintained from the Storm\nPrediction Center. \n\nBeyond Friday, this pattern likely continues through at least\nthe early and middle portions of next week. South to \nsouthwesterly flow is maintained over the Mid-Atlantic. Hot and \nhumid conditions each afternoon, with highs reaching the upper \n80s to low 90s, and heat indices in the low 100s each of the \nBlue Ridge. Scattered showers and thunderstorms each afternoon.\nDeep layer shear is forecast to increase through the weekend as\nthe subtropical jet stream strengthens. On Saturday, a more \npotent shortwave trough is forecast to move overhead that could \naid in stronger, more organized severe convection. Damaging wind\ngusts will be the main threat.\n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft will develop Sunday into into next week. \nSunday could bring a short reprieve as dry conditions prevail.\nHowever, seasonably hot and humid conditions continue, and\nshould quickly bring showers and storms back into the forecast \nMonday through most of next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy dense fog will be possible over the next several\nmornings.\n\nWith daily showers and thunderstorms, and warm/humid overnights,\npatchy dense fog can be expected each morning. The location \nwill be difficult to pinpoint, with areas that receive rain the \nday prior the likely candidates. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front could arrive late in the week, \npotentially bringing a pattern change into next weekend. \n\nWhile this is very far out in the forecast period, there is some\nsignal that a more potent frontal system could push through the\nregion late in the week. The details are murky at this stage of\nthe forecast, with timing very uncertain, as well as potential\nimpacts. Guidance is generally between Thursday/Friday right\nnow, with improvement in the weather pattern possible \nthereafter. Any decent frontal passage in the summer time should\nbe watched closely for potential severe weather, so we will \nkeep monitoring this in the coming days to see if that potential\nis there, and try to narrow down the timeframe a bit more.",
